To declare inheritance in Python, write the child class name followed by a parent class name in parentheses. The general syntax is: class ChildClassName(ParentClassName):. You can also inherit from multiple parent classes by listing them separated with commas, such as class Child(ParentA, ParentB):. Inside the child class, you can access parent attributes and methods directly. If the child defines a method with the same name as a parent method, it overrides the parent's version. Here is a simple example:

class Vehicle:
    def start_engine(self):
        print('engine started')

class Car(Vehicle):
    def honk(self):
        print('beep')

c = Car()
c.start_engine()  # inherited from Vehicle
c.honk()          # defined in Car

